[1] user: Implement Gitea issue #783 for the Sport Analytics Tool / Stat'sTheGame repository.

IMPORTANT PROJECT RULES

Before making any changes:

1. Read and follow all applicable repository instructions, especially:
   - AGENTS.md / AGENTS.override.md if present;
   - docs/git-methodology.md;
   - docs/ai/usage.md or the current AI policy guidance;
   - docs/testing/user-testing-protocol.md;
   - docs/testing/user-testing-task-bank.md;
   - docs/api/consumer-keys.md;
   - docs/api/overview.md;
   - docs/api/openapi.md / docs/api/openapi.yaml;
   - relevant frontend/API Explorer documentation;
   - evidence/user-testing/sprint-3/2026-09-28-P13-api-consumer.md if it is present on main.

2. This project uses Gitea as the authoritative repository.
   - Do NOT commit or push to the GitHub mirror.
   - Do NOT commit directly to main.
   - Do NOT push directly to main.
   - Multiple developers are working on the repository, so do not reset, discard, overwrite or reformat unrelated work.

3. Follow the project AI policy and add the required AI attribution to changed documentation / AI register and eventually the commit message.

ISSUE

#783 — Document how external API consumers request access and obtain an API key

USER-TESTING CONTEXT

This issue was created from Sprint 3 formal user-testing finding:

- User-feedback issue: #612
- Participant: P13
- Scenario: S3-API-ADV-01
- Task: PUB-05
- Finding: P13-F01
- Severity: S2
- Decision: Accept

The participant could easily discover the API page and understand available API operations, but could not determine how a legitimate external consumer is supposed to request access or obtain an API key.

The participant repeated the same concern in the post-test questions.

Because this is an accepted S2 finding, #612 must remain open until:
1. #783 is implemented;
2. the corrected build is deployed; and
3. PUB-05 is formally retested.

Do NOT fabricate or record a successful retest as part of this implementation.

GOAL

A prospective external developer who discovers the Stat'sTheGame API should be able to understand, without facilitator assistance:

- which operations are available anonymously;
- which operations require a consumer API key;
- that consumer API keys are administrator-issued;
- how a legitimate external consumer should request access under the project's current model;
- how the issued key is used;
- how the key must be handled securely;
- where to find the detailed consumer API documentation.

The user must not need to infer this from administrator-only pages.

IMPORTANT PRODUCT BOUNDARY

Do NOT implement self-service API-key issuance unless the current repository explicitly says that self-service consumer registration is an approved requirement.

The current model is administrator-issued API consumer keys.

Do NOT:
- expose administrator-only controls publicly;
- expose raw keys;
- expose API key hashes;
- put API keys into URLs or query strings;
- add fake signup/contact functionality;
- invent an email address, support portal or request form that does not exist;
- change authentication/authorization architecture without a separately approved requirement.

If there is no existing dedicated contact/request mechanism in the repository, clearly document the current process in product-neutral language such as requesting API consumer access from a Stat'sTheGame administrator/project administrator, rather than inventing a contact channel.

FIRST: INVESTIGATE THE CURRENT IMPLEMENTATION

Before editing, inspect and report:

1. How `/api` is currently implemented in the frontend.
2. How the API Explorer/OpenAPI UI is presented.
3. What public API introductory text currently exists.
4. Where `docs/api/consumer-keys.md` is linked, if anywhere.
5. Whether the frontend already distinguishes:
   - Public anonymous API;
   - Consumer API;
   - application/admin API.
6. Whether any existing UI or docs already explain how keys are issued.
7. Whether the project's navigation has a <truncated omitted_approx_tokens="1038" />12. The implementation leaves #612 open and does not claim that PUB-05 has been retested.

TESTING

Add or update focused tests covering at least:

- API page renders consumer-access guidance;
- consumer API is identified as requiring an API key;
- keys are identified as administrator-issued;
- `X-API-Key` usage is explained;
- public vs consumer access is distinguishable;
- link to consumer documentation works or points to the intended destination;
- no raw key/secret fixture is rendered;
- existing API Explorer still loads;
- accessibility basics remain intact.

If this project already has Playwright coverage for the API Explorer, extend the most appropriate existing journey rather than creating redundant test infrastructure.

For browser/e2e coverage, verify on:
- desktop Chromium;
- the project's normal mobile viewport (for example Pixel 7 if that is the existing convention);
- no horizontal overflow;
- Axe/accessibility checks if the existing suite uses them.

QUALITY CHECKS

Run the relevant checks for all changed work.

At minimum:

npm run format:check
npm run lint
npm run typecheck
npm run test:frontend
npm run openapi:lint   # only if OpenAPI/docs contract changes warrant it
npm run build --workspace=@sport-analytics/frontend
git diff --check

Run focused tests first, then broader repository checks where practical.

If `npm run check` is appropriate, run it.

Do not hide unrelated failures. Report them separately and verify whether they reproduce independently.

GIT WORKFLOW

First inspect:

git status
git branch --show-current
git remote -v
git log --oneline --decorate -10

Ensure the authoritative remote is Gitea.

Start from the latest clean main:

git switch main
git pull --ff-only origin main

Then create:

feat/783-api-consumer-onboarding

If unrelated local work exists, stop and report it rather than discarding it.

Do not commit until I explicitly approve the completed implementation.

Do not push or create a PR unless I explicitly ask.

USER-TESTING EVIDENCE

If the P13 session evidence and #612 summary are already merged into main:

- preserve them;
- do not change the historical P13 task result from Partial to Success;
- do not mark P13-F01 as resolved merely because code exists;
- do not mark #612 Accepted;
- do not fabricate a retest.

You MAY update #783 references/documentation if necessary, but formal resolution requires a deployed PUB-05 retest.

If those evidence files are not yet on main, do not recreate or duplicate them on #783.

SECURITY REVIEW

Before completion explicitly verify:

- no API key appears in frontend source;
- no API key appears in docs examples except clearly fake placeholders;
- no raw secret is logged;
- no admin bearer token is exposed;
- no new public key-issuance endpoint was created;
- admin issuance remains server-side/admin-authorized;
- API-key onboarding guidance does not imply more permissions than consumer keys actually have.

COMPLETION REPORT

When implementation is complete, give me:

1. branch name;
2. current git status;
3. exact files changed;
4. concise explanation of the implemented user journey;
5. what a prospective consumer now sees on `/api`;
6. how the access-request process is described;
7. why the wording matches the current administrator-issued model;
8. whether any API/backend/OpenAPI behavior changed;
9. tests added/updated;
10. all commands run and exact pass/fail results;
11. accessibility/mobile verification;
12. security/privacy review;
13. documentation updated;
14. AI policy/register updates;
15. any unrelated failures or limitations;
16. confirmation that #612/PUB-05 retest has NOT been fabricated;
17. whether the build is technically ready for deployed PUB-05 retesting.

Also provide a suggested commit message:

feat(api): clarify consumer API onboarding

Refs #783
Refs #612
Assisted-by: Codex[ACTUAL_MODEL]

And a suggested PR title:

#783 Clarify external API consumer onboarding

Do not commit, push or create the PR yet.

